{
  "requirement": "The enabled Habeas Symposium plugin MUST request a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI and use that installation as its session-start hook command, so the CLI is available when the hook advises an agent to run `habeas setup`.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The Cargo.toml content needed for this requirement exists, but the `{impl}` marker is attached to the wrong node, and nothing is marked `{test}` for the requirement.\n\n**What the requirement needs.**\n- `[[package.metadata.symposium.installations]]` (Cargo.toml lines 24–31) requests a global Cargo install: `source = \"cargo\"`, `crate = \"habeas\"`, `executable = \"habeas\"`, `global = true`.\n- `[[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]]` (lines 32–38) uses `command = \"habeas\"` with `args = [\"hook\", \"session-start\"]` for the `SessionStart` event.\n- Together, these two tables are the substance of the requirement.\n\n**Where the marker sits.**\n- The only `{impl}` reference for `requirements.integration.symposium_cli` is the comment at Cargo.toml line 23. The outline tool places it inside `table[package.metadata.symposium.skills]` (lines 20–23).\n- That table only declares `source.path = \"assets/skills\"`. It has nothing to do with a global CLI installation or the hook command.\n- The comments appear to be written to annotate the table that follows them. The parser treats them as trailing content of the preceding table.\n- The marker on line 31 (`symposium_setup_hook`) is attached to the installations table, and the hooks table carries no marker at all.\n- The installations table and hooks table, which implement this requirement, therefore have no `{impl}` reference to it. The marked node implements none of it. Under the criteria, that is both an unmarked implementation and a misattributed one.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `references` shows no `{test}` or `{cite}` site for `symposium_cli`.\n- `tests/symposium_hook_smoke.py` does assert that the exact Cargo installation declaration (`source = \"cargo\"`, `crate`, `executable`, `global = true`) is present. It also exercises hook dispatch. But it is marked only with `symposium_setup_hook` and `symposium`.\n- Nothing is marked as verifying this requirement. Nothing checks that the installation declaration is global, or that the hook command matches the installed executable.\n\nFixing this needs two changes:\n- Move or add the `{impl}` marker so it annotates the installations and hooks tables.\n- Add a `{test}` reference, for example on the smoke test's installation-declaration check.",
